1.  Why is a proximity matrix symmetric and hollow in the standard setting?

    -   The proximity matrix is hollow since the distance between an observation and itself equals 0.

    -   It is symmetric since the distance between the same pairs of observations is the same across the rows and columns of the matrix. Thus transpose(X)=X

2.  Why can the axes of an MDS map not be interpreted in the same way as original measured variables?\
    The coordinates of MDS are merely one of the many possible coordinates that preserve the pairwise dissimilarities; they do not have any intrinsic value outside their relative positions and inter-point distances

3.  At what stage should variables be standardised when MDS begins from a data matrix?\
    The feature values need to be standardized before the distance matrix is computed

4.  What information does non-metric MDS preserve that metric MDS may not prioritise?\
    Non-metric MDS prioritizes maintaining the distance rank of points whilst metric MDS prioritizes maintaining the numeric distance differences between points observation i.e getting those numeric differences as close as possible to the original input distances

5.  What would a poor Shepard diagram look like?\
    The points in a poor Shepard diagram would show configuration distances (\$d\_{ij}\$) lying very far from the monotonically increasing fitted relationship /curve

6.  Why is MDS not itself a solution to curved or circular structure?

    MDS is designed to preserve given pairwise dissimilarites. If the data lies on a curved structure, Euclidean straight line distances may not fully or appropriately represent the underlying structure or distance between points along that structure- unless the supplied distances are a good approximation of the true geodesic distance between points enter ISOMAP.
